# Universal Academic Research Paper Cursor Rules

## Document Structure & Formatting

### LaTeX Document Setup
- Use appropriate journal/conference document class
- Include necessary packages for figures, tables, math, and bibliography
- Follow target venue's formatting guidelines and style requirements

### Document Organization
- **Title Page**: Include complete title, author information, affiliations, and abstract
- **Keywords**: Provide 3-10 relevant keywords
- **Sections**: Introduction, Literature Review, Methodology, Results, Discussion, Conclusion
- **References**: Use appropriate bibliography style (APA, IEEE, ACM, etc.)
- **Appendices**: Include supplementary materials as needed

### File Structure
- Use descriptive filenames (e.g., `paper.tex`, `references.bib`)
- Separate content from formatting when possible
- Maintain version control with clear commit messages

## Writing Style & Academic Standards

### Language & Tone
- Maintain formal, objective, academic tone throughout
- Use third-person perspective; avoid first-person pronouns
- Write clearly and concisely; avoid unnecessary jargon
- Present findings factually without overstating significance
- Use active voice when possible, passive when appropriate

### Content Organization
- **Introduction**: State problem, significance, objectives, and contributions
- **Literature Review**: Summarize relevant work, identify gaps
- **Methodology**: Detail approach, data collection, analysis methods
- **Results**: Present findings with appropriate visualizations
- **Discussion**: Interpret results, relate to literature, address limitations
- **Conclusion**: Summarize contributions, suggest future work

### Technical Writing
- Define all acronyms and abbreviations on first use
- Use consistent terminology throughout
- Maintain appropriate level of technical detail for target audience
- Explain complex concepts clearly

### Abbreviations & Acronyms
- Spell out abbreviations/acronyms completely on first mention followed by abbreviation in parentheses: "Security Edge Protection Proxy (SEPP)"
- Use abbreviated form consistently in subsequent mentions: "SEPP interface"
- Maintain consistent abbreviation usage throughout the document
- Avoid over-abbreviation; only abbreviate terms used frequently

## Citation & Referencing

### Citation Style
- Choose appropriate style (APA, IEEE, ACM, Vancouver, etc.) for field/venue
- Cite sources appropriately: inline citations, footnotes, or endnotes
- Ensure all references are cited in text and vice versa
- Use tools like Zotero, Mendeley, or BibTeX for management

### Reference Formatting
- Include all required fields (authors, title, journal, year, DOI, etc.)
- Format references consistently according to chosen style
- Verify all DOIs and URLs are accessible
- Cross-reference related work appropriately

## Tables & Figures

### Table Guidelines
- Use appropriate table environment (`tabular`, `tabularx`, `longtable`)
- Include descriptive captions above tables
- Label tables for cross-referencing (`\label{tab:label}`)
- Use `\toprule`, `\midrule`, `\bottomrule` for professional appearance
- Ensure tables are self-contained and interpretable

### Figure Guidelines
- Use appropriate figure environment with placement options
- Include descriptive captions below figures
- Label figures for cross-referencing (`\label{fig:label}`)
- Use vector formats (PDF, EPS) when possible
- Ensure figures are high-resolution and legible
- Cite figure sources if not original

### Table & Figure Referencing
- Reference tables and figures in text using proper LaTeX syntax: `Table~\ref{tab:label}` and `Figure~\ref{fig:label}`
- Use tilde (~) before `\ref{}` to prevent line breaks between "Table"/"Figure" and the number
- Introduce tables/figures before referencing: "As shown in Table~\ref{tab:results}..." or "The experimental setup is illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:setup}"
- Place references after the introductory text but before detailed descriptions
- Ensure all referenced tables and figures exist and are properly numbered

## Mathematical Content

### Equations
- Use appropriate equation environments (`equation`, `align`, `gather`)
- Number equations for cross-referencing
- Use consistent notation and symbols
- Explain variables and parameters clearly
- Format mathematical expressions professionally

### Algorithms
- Use algorithm packages (`algorithm`, `algpseudocode`)
- Provide clear, step-by-step descriptions
- Include proper indentation and structure
- Label algorithms for cross-referencing
- Test pseudocode for logical correctness

## Code & Technical Elements

### Code Formatting
- Use `\texttt{}` or `listings` package for code snippets
- Apply syntax highlighting where appropriate
- Include comments for complex code sections
- Ensure code is syntactically correct
- Provide code repositories or supplements when possible

### Technical Specifications
- Format units consistently (MB, GB, ms, MHz, etc.)
- Use appropriate numerical precision
- Format IP addresses, URLs, and technical identifiers consistently
- Include version numbers for software and tools
- Specify hardware configurations clearly

## Quality Assurance & Validation

### Consistency Checks
- Verify all abbreviations are defined before use
- Ensure consistent terminology and spelling
- Check table/figure numbering and references
- Validate internal consistency across sections
- Use spell-checking and grammar tools

### Technical Validation
- Verify mathematical equations and derivations
- Test algorithms and code snippets
- Ensure experimental results are reproducible
- Validate statistical analyses and methodologies
- Cross-check with established literature

### Peer Review Preparation
- Anticipate common reviewer concerns
- Address potential limitations proactively
- Ensure sufficient detail for reproducibility
- Prepare response to potential criticisms
- Follow venue-specific submission requirements

## Research Ethics & Integrity

### Academic Integrity
- Cite all sources appropriately
- Avoid plagiarism in all forms
- Acknowledge collaborators and contributors
- Disclose conflicts of interest
- Maintain research data integrity

### Reproducibility
- Provide sufficient methodological detail
- Share data and code when possible
- Document experimental setups clearly
- Include necessary supplementary materials
- Enable verification of results

## Version Control & Collaboration

### Change Tracking
- Use meaningful commit messages
- Track major revisions and rationale
- Maintain backup versions
- Document collaborative contributions
- Preserve research timeline

### Collaboration Guidelines
- Establish clear authorship criteria
- Define roles and responsibilities
- Use collaborative writing tools effectively
- Maintain regular communication
- Respect intellectual property rights

## Field-Specific Considerations

### Adapt to Research Domain
- Follow domain-specific conventions (CS vs. Biology vs. Physics)
- Use appropriate jargon and terminology
- Adhere to field-specific citation practices
- Consider domain-specific ethical requirements
- Follow venue-specific formatting requirements

### Publication Strategy
- Identify appropriate target venues
- Tailor content to audience expectations
- Consider journal/conference review processes
- Plan for potential revisions
- Prepare for post-publication engagement
